## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of MVCC in a distributed, sharded MongoDB setup and how to enforce ACID-like behavior for per-user quotas under concurrent updates. It also probes the choice between transactions vs atomic updates and retry semantics.

## Key Concepts
- MVCC and snapshot isolation in distributed transactions
- Multi-document transactions across shards
- Read/Write concerns and retryable transactions
- Idempotency and auditing in failure scenarios

## Code Example
```javascript
// Node.js pseudo
const session = client.startSession();
try {
  await session.withTransaction(async () => {
    const doc = await coll.findOne({ userId }, { session, readConcern: { level: 'snapshot' } });
    const amt = 3;
    if ((doc.quotas.featureA || 0) < amt) throw new Error('Insufficient quota');
    await coll.updateOne({ userId }, { $inc: { 'quotas.featureA': -amt } }, { session });
    await auditCol.insertOne({ userId, feature: 'featureA', delta: -amt, ts: new Date() }, { session });
  }, { readConcern: { level: 'snapshot' }, writeConcern: { w: 'majority' } });
} catch (e) {
  // handle retry at caller level
}
